What is a plot?

A plot is a visualization of data. There are several types of plots that can be used to visualize data, including: boxplots, histograms, scatter plots, and line plots. Plots can be used to identify patterns, trends, and outliers in data.



```{r}
#| message: false
#| include: false
library(tidyverse)
library(ggplot2)
data <- read.csv("bikedata.csv")
```

```{r}
#| echo: false
ggplot(data = data, 
       aes(x = diesel_price, 
           fill = semester_break)) +
  geom_boxplot()
```


```{r}
ggplot(data = data, 
       aes(x = temperature)) +
  geom_histogram(binwidth = 3, col = "cornflowerblue", fill = "coral")
```

```{r}
data %>% 
  filter(!(weekday %in% c("Sat", "Sun")),
         holiday == F) %>% 
  ggplot(aes(x = temperature, y = cyclists)) +
  geom_jitter(aes(col = weekday, alpha = 0.4, size = 0.2)) +
  xlab("Tempeeeeeeeeeeeeeeeerature")+
  ylab("Cyclists(z.B. Annika)") + 
  theme_minimal()
```








